<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Widget card geometry.

  ── THE INSET ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A widget's host view IS its cell: measured on a Pixel launcher, a 4x1
  Mihrab widget gets [50,966]–[1030,1227] and the next row starts where
  that one ends. Nothing separates two widgets except what they choose not
  to paint. These widgets painted edge to edge, so two of them stacked
  fused into one slab with a seam through it — the report that started
  this — and because the fill is translucent (88% by default) any pixel of
  overlap composited twice and drew a dark line.

  So the card is inset. The gap belongs to the widget, not to the
  launcher, because the launcher is not reliably providing one.

  Kept small deliberately: 6dp on each side of a shared edge reads as 12dp
  between two cards, which is about the spacing between the app's own
  cards inside the app, and it costs 12dp of a ~330dp-wide widget.

  ── THE RADIUS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  16dp is the pre-Android-12 house value. On API 31+ `values-v31`
  overrides it with the platform's own widget radius, which is the number
  the launcher's corner enforcement uses — matching it means our corners
  and any mask the launcher applies land on top of each other instead of
  one showing inside the other.
-->
<resources>
    <dimen name="widget_card_inset">6dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="widget_card_radius">16dp</dimen>
</resources>
